<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Estado Novo (Brazil)</h1><p class="st-meta">1937 CE – 1946 CE · capital Rio de Janeiro</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Getúlio Vargas's authoritarian New State, which built modern Brazil's labour laws and heavy industry and sent troops to fight in Europe.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>On 10 November 1937 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/getulio-vargas">Getúlio Vargas</a>, president of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/second-brazilian-republic">Second Brazilian Republic</a>, closed Congress with army support and imposed a new constitution modelled on European authoritarian regimes, creating the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estado-novo-portugal">Estado Novo</a>, or New State. Elections planned for 1938 were cancelled.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/getulio-vargas">Vargas</a> ruled by decree, abolished political parties and censored the press through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">Department</a> of Press and Propaganda. He centralised power, burning state flags in a public ceremony. He introduced a minimum wage in 1940 and the Consolidation of Labour Laws in 1943, winning working-class support. The state founded the National <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">Steel</a> Company at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volta-redonda">Volta Redonda</a>. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/new-republic-brazil">Brazil</a> joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> in 1942 and sent an expeditionary force to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 1944.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>As the war ended, pressure for democracy grew, and the military deposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/getulio-vargas">Vargas</a> on 29 October 1945. Elections followed, and the constitution of 1946 created the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/fourth-brazilian-republic">Fourth Brazilian Republic</a>. Vargas returned to power by election in 1951.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/estado-novo-brazil.jpg" alt="Estado Novo (Brazil)" width="320" height="224"><figcaption>Image: Raimundo Teixeira Mendes · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Brazil_(1889%E2%80%931960).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1937 CE – 1946 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Rio de Janeiro</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Getúlio Vargas</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Portuguese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Brazil</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Third Brazilian Republic, New State (Brazil)</dd></dl></aside></div>
